Do not give the redo worker a dynamic shared memory control segment.

A walredo worker gets killed by its own seccomp filter every so often,
on unlink (zou #332). The caller turns out to be its own exit:

    unlink("/dev/shm/PostgreSQL.554764962")
     > shm_unlink
     > dsm_impl_posix
     > dsm_postmaster_shutdown
     > shmem_exit
     > proc_exit
     > ZouRedoMain

The worker is a standalone process, so CreateSharedMemoryAndSemaphores
runs dsm_postmaster_startup for it and it ends up owning a control
segment nobody else can see. Destroying that on the way out is a
shm_unlink, which glibc implements as unlink on a path under /dev/shm,
and unlink is not on the sandbox allowlist. It should not be either:
the worker replays records it did not write, and a process that can
unlink by name can delete the data directory and the store.

It was rare because the pool kills a retiring worker rather than
waiting for it, so the SIGKILL usually wins the race with proc_exit.
Feeding a worker a clean EOF reproduces it every time.

Nothing in the worker has any use for dynamic shared memory: it runs
no parallel workers and loads no extensions. So it does not create the
control segment at all, which also stops it leaking one under /dev/shm
every time the pool kills it. dsm_create says what is going on instead
of dereferencing the NULL control pointer, in case a future caller
appears.

diff --git a/src/backend/storage/ipc/ipci.c b/src/backend/storage/ipc/ipci.c
--- a/src/backend/storage/ipc/ipci.c
+++ b/src/backend/storage/ipc/ipci.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include "access/xlogprefetcher.h"
 #include "access/xlogrecovery.h"
 #include "access/zou_wal.h"
+#include "access/zou_walredo.h"
 #include "commands/async.h"
 #include "miscadmin.h"
 #include "pgstat.h"
@@ -243,8 +244,18 @@
 	/* Initialize subsystems */
 	CreateOrAttachShmemStructs();
 
-	/* Initialize dynamic shared memory facilities. */
-	dsm_postmaster_startup(shim);
+	/*
+	 * Initialize dynamic shared memory facilities.
+	 *
+	 * The zou redo worker is skipped.  It is a standalone process, so it
+	 * would create a control segment of its own and destroy it again on the
+	 * way out, and the destroy is a shm_unlink, which is an unlink, which is
+	 * not a syscall the redo sandbox allows.  The worker has no use for
+	 * dynamic shared memory anyway: nothing in it runs parallel workers or
+	 * loads an extension.
+	 */
+	if (!zou_redo_active)
+		dsm_postmaster_startup(shim);
 
 	/*
 	 * Now give loadable modules a chance to set up their shmem allocations
diff --git a/src/backend/storage/ipc/dsm.c b/src/backend/storage/ipc/dsm.c
--- a/src/backend/storage/ipc/dsm.c
+++ b/src/backend/storage/ipc/dsm.c
@@ -533,6 +533,17 @@
 	if (!dsm_init_done)
 		dsm_backend_startup();
 
+	/*
+	 * A process that skipped dsm_postmaster_startup has no control segment to
+	 * record this in.  Only the zou redo worker does that, and it is meant to
+	 * have no dsm user at all, so say so rather than dereferencing NULL a few
+	 * lines down.
+	 */
+	if (dsm_control == NULL)
+		ereport(ERROR,
+				(errcode(ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED),
+				 errmsg("dynamic shared memory is not available in this process")));
+
 	/* Create a new segment descriptor. */
 	seg = dsm_create_descriptor();
 
